---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import SectionIntro from "../../components/SectionIntro.astro";
import { basicsCards } from "../../data/content";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="TCM Basics | HerbGuide"
  description="Learn core Traditional Chinese Medicine ideas in clear, careful English without overpromising or overcomplicating."
  structuredData={[
    {
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "CollectionPage",
      name: "TCM Basics",
      description:
        "Plain-English introductions to core Traditional Chinese Medicine terms and concepts for general educational reading.",
      url: "https://herbguide.store/basics/",
    },
    {
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is qi a scientific term?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. On HerbGuide, qi is explained as a traditional concept inside Chinese medicine, usually translated into plain English ideas like function, activity, resilience, or vitality.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does dampness mean water retention?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Not exactly. Dampness is a broader traditional pattern term used inside Chinese medicine, and it should not be reduced to one modern medical label.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why do Chinese herb pages list channels?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Channel entry helps show how a herb is categorized in TCM theory. It gives readers context for traditional usage, even though it does not map directly to anatomy.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
  ]}
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="shell narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">TCM Basics</p>
      <h1>Core ideas in plain English</h1>
      <p>
        We translate Chinese medicine concepts into practical explanations while keeping
        clear boundaries between traditional theory and modern medical advice.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <SectionIntro
        title="Start here"
        text="These pages are designed to reduce confusion for new readers and give clearer context for herb and recipe content."
      />
      <div class="card-grid">
        {basicsCards.map((card) => (
          <article class="card">
            <h2><a href={`/basics/${card.slug}/`}>{card.title}</a></h2>
            <p>{card.text}</p>
            <p class="card-link"><a href={`/basics/${card.slug}/`}>Read article</a></p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="shell two-up">
      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Plain-English frame</p>
        <h2>What TCM means on this site</h2>
        <p>
          Traditional Chinese Medicine, often shortened to TCM, is a long-running body of
          ideas about the body, daily habits, food, herbs, climate, and patterns of
          imbalance. It has its own vocabulary, and that vocabulary does not always match
          modern biomedical language one-to-one.
        </p>
        <p>
          On HerbGuide, we treat TCM as a cultural and educational framework. We explain
          how a traditional herb is described, where that language comes from, and how food
          traditions are commonly discussed in Chinese culture. We do not present those
          ideas as personal instructions or health promises.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Important boundary</p>
        <h2>How to read traditional terms responsibly</h2>
        <p>
          Words like <strong>qi</strong>, <strong>dampness</strong>, <strong>yin</strong>,
          and <strong>heat</strong> are best understood as parts of a traditional model.
          They are not direct diagnostic labels, lab values, or guaranteed explanations for
          personal experiences.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Qi</strong> is often explained as functional energy, activity, or vitality.</li>
          <li><strong>Blood</strong> in TCM is broader than literal blood in a lab report.</li>
          <li><strong>Dampness</strong> usually describes heaviness, sluggishness, or turbid accumulation in traditional theory.</li>
          <li><strong>Channels</strong> are functional pathways in TCM, not the same thing as anatomical organs or blood vessels.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell two-up">
      <article class="prose-block list-card">
        <h2>What does qi mean in Traditional Chinese Medicine?</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Qi</strong> is one of the first Chinese medicine words many readers
          encounter, and it is also one of the easiest to misunderstand. A practical
          translation is <em>functional vitality</em> or the body's capacity to do what it
          needs to do: warm, move, transform, protect, and recover.
        </p>
        <p>
          When TCM writing says a herb relates to qi, the site will usually translate that
          into everyday language such as stamina, appetite, recovery, resilience, or
          general strength, while still making it clear that this is traditional language
          rather than a medical claim.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-block list-card">
        <h2>What are warming and cooling foods in TCM?</h2>
        <p>
          Warming and cooling are traditional qualities, not temperature alone. A cooling
          herb can be served warm as tea. A warming ingredient can appear in a room
          temperature dish. The point is the <em>traditional effect profile</em>, not simply
          whether the food is hot or cold to the touch.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is why ginger, chrysanthemum, mint, and pear often get discussed so
          differently in Chinese food traditions. HerbGuide keeps translating these labels
          into simple, practical language instead of assuming the reader already knows the
          system.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="shell two-up">
      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Useful comparisons</p>
        <h2>Chinese herbs compared in plain English</h2>
        <p>
          Some readers are not looking for a definition page. They want a clear comparison:
          fresh ginger vs dried ginger, ginseng vs American ginseng, or goji berry vs jujube.
          These are strong search patterns because they combine beginner curiosity with real
          ingredient intent.
        </p>
        <p>
          Digestive comparisons are also especially helpful for readers who land on chen pi,
          hawthorn, poria, or Job's Tears first.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Best next reads</p>
        <h2>Start with these side-by-side guides</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="/basics/fresh-ginger-vs-dried-ginger/">Fresh Ginger vs Dried Ginger</a></li>
          <li><a href="/basics/ginseng-vs-american-ginseng/">Ginseng vs American Ginseng</a></li>
          <li><a href="/basics/goji-berry-vs-jujube/">Goji Berry vs Jujube</a></li>
          <li><a href="/basics/chen-pi-vs-hawthorn-berry/">Chen Pi vs Hawthorn Berry</a></li>
          <li><a href="/basics/poria-vs-jobs-tears/">Poria vs Job's Tears</a></li>
          <li><a href="/basics/chrysanthemum-vs-mint/">Chrysanthemum vs Mint</a></li>
          <li><a href="/basics/goji-berry-tea-vs-goji-berry-porridge/">Goji Berry Tea vs Goji Berry Porridge</a></li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="shell two-up">
      <article class="prose-block list-card">
        <h2>What does dampness mean in Chinese medicine?</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Dampness</strong> is a classic example of a TCM term that sounds strange
          in English. It does not literally mean the body is wet. In plain English, it
          often points to patterns of heaviness, stickiness, cloudiness, sluggish
          digestion, or a sense that things are not transforming smoothly.
        </p>
        <p>
          That is why grains, soups, aromatic peels, and certain lighter foods often appear
          in traditional discussions of dampness. The site keeps that language general and
          educational rather than turning it into personal guidance.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-block list-card">
        <h2>What does channel entry mean for Chinese herbs?</h2>
        <p>
          When a herb is said to enter the Lung, Spleen, or Liver channel, that does not
          mean it only affects those literal organs in a modern anatomical sense. In TCM,
          organ names often represent broader functional networks.
        </p>
        <p>
          For example, the TCM <strong>Spleen</strong> system has a lot to do with
          transformation of food, fluids, and daily nourishment. The TCM
          <strong>Liver</strong> system often appears in discussions of flow, tension, and
          stored blood. HerbGuide explains these terms directly on herb pages so readers are
          not left guessing.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell two-up">
      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Plain-English help</p>
        <h2>Traditional Chinese Medicine terms explained in simple English</h2>
        <p>
          Many readers come here with practical questions like what qi means in simple
          English, what dampness means in TCM, and how to understand warming and cooling
          foods without clinical jargon.
        </p>
        <p>
          The purpose of HerbGuide is to help readers understand the language first so they
          can read herb and food tradition content more confidently.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Next steps</p>
        <h2>How these basics connect to herb pages</h2>
        <p>
          Once a reader understands qi, dampness, warming, cooling, and channel entry, the
          herb library becomes much easier to navigate. Terms on pages for astragalus, goji
          berry, chrysanthemum, jujube, ginger, and poria stop looking mysterious and start
          looking like a consistent vocabulary.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="shell two-up">
      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Reader questions</p>
        <h2>TCM basics FAQ</h2>
        <h3>Is qi a scientific term?</h3>
        <p>
          No. On HerbGuide, qi is explained as a traditional concept inside Chinese
          medicine, usually translated into plain English ideas like function, activity,
          resilience, or vitality.
        </p>
        <h3>Does dampness mean water retention?</h3>
        <p>
          Not exactly. Dampness is a broader traditional pattern term. Sometimes readers
          associate it with heaviness or sluggishness, but it should not be reduced to one
          modern medical label.
        </p>
        <h3>Why do Chinese herb pages list channels?</h3>
        <p>
          Channel entry helps show how a herb is categorized in TCM theory. It gives
          readers context for traditional usage, even though it does not map directly to
          anatomy.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Keep exploring</p>
        <h2>Herb pages that make these terms easier to understand</h2>
        <p>
          For qi-related reading, compare
          <a href="/herbs/astragalus-root/">Astragalus Root</a>,
          <a href="/herbs/ginseng/">Ginseng</a>, and
          <a href="/herbs/codonopsis/">Codonopsis</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          For warming and cooling ideas, compare
          <a href="/herbs/fresh-ginger/">Fresh Ginger</a>,
          <a href="/herbs/dried-ginger/">Dried Ginger</a>,
          <a href="/herbs/chrysanthemum-flower/">Chrysanthemum</a>, and
          <a href="/herbs/mint/">Mint</a>.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
